{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001","f002"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s08; BRANCH=peer-trusted-transport; Search only snapshots/s08. Cross-subsystem trust-boundary analogue completing this snapshot's matrix row: find kernel-side clients or servers of remote or virtual device transports — network-exported buses, virtual HCDs, guest-host channels, remote-storage/remote-fs reply handlers (9p trans_fd/virtio/rdma/xen, nbd), greybus/serdev links, virtio/vhost ring protocols, remote admin-queue or DMA reply handlers — where allocation or iteration is driven by peer-supplied response counts/lengths, and check whether those peer fields are cross-checked against locally-known limits before use. Prior rounds only enumerated 9p, nbd, virtio/vhost, and HID transports without deep-reading them: give those a real read here, including completion-time peer metadata such as urb->actual_length handed to memcpy or iteration bounds. Identify by mechanism (peer-controlled size crossing a trust boundary into kernel allocation or loop), including callers/callees and alternative protocol framings, not by subsystem name. Record the peer-field source, the missing local limit check, and the consuming allocation/loop; a negative result must list the peer-driven handlers examined. The seed usbip path is demonstration-only and must never be reported. Save a structured artifact (execution_status, candidates or evidence-backed negative) for snapshots/s08 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s09; BRANCH=alloc-multiply-overflow; Search only snapshots/s09. Direct structural/API analogue of the seed's sizing chain: hunt a 32-bit multiply (count*element-size, count+offset, or count<<shift) computed with plain int/unsigned arithmetic from an untrusted count and passed to kmalloc/kzalloc/vmalloc/kmemdup/devm_kzalloc instead of kcalloc/array_size/struct_size, followed by an element-indexed copy/recv/memcpy loop that still trusts the original count, so the truncated product under-allocates and the loop writes past the heap buffer. Sweep driver, fs, and net parsers within this one snapshot; prioritize counts that cross a trust boundary (wire parse, ioctl, device/DMA metadata, on-disk structures) over locally computed counts. For each candidate record file:line of the unchecked arithmetic, the allocator call, the consuming loop, and where the count enters from outside the kernel; a negative result must cite the allocator-adjacent arithmetic sites examined. The seed usbip_recv_iso chain itself is demonstration-only and must never be reported; if unpatched usbip code is present, record it as excluded-by-rule and keep searching. Save a structured artifact (execution_status, candidates or evidence-backed negative) for snapshots/s09 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001","f004"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s09; BRANCH=unclamped-wire-count; Search only snapshots/s09. Equivalent data-flow and validation invariant with different names: trace deserialization entry points (protocol unpack/recvmsg, netlink nla_parse, ioctl copy_from_user, nlmsg, message parsers) that store a count/length/nr_entries/nr_segs/nr_bufs value from the wire or userspace into a persistent kernel object with no range check against a known maximum; prioritize asymmetric validation where a sibling path of the same protocol enforces a MAX or #define limit (request vs reply, submit vs return, in vs out direction) but the mirror path does not. Do not stop at the usbip family: it is the seed and demonstration-only, so if the unpatched usbip store is present, record it as excluded-by-rule and sweep the rest of the tree (drivers, net, fs, sound, block, crypto). For each candidate record the parse site, the missing clamp, the enforcing sibling path, and the downstream consumer; a negative result must list the parse entry points verified as clamped. Save a structured artifact for snapshots/s09 only."}
{"event":"intent","sources":["origin","f001","f003","f005"],"description":"SNAPSHOT=s09; BRANCH=error-unwind-poisoned-count; Search only snapshots/s09. Lifetime/ownership/state-transition analogue: audit early-return, failure, abort, and teardown paths that leave an attacker-controlled count/length/actual_size/num field stored in a shared object (urb, request, skb, scatterlist, iovec, ring entry, transaction descriptor) which a later completion handler, cleanup routine, or status-out/error-unwind path iterates, causing OOB access during error handling even when the happy path is safe. Prior audits left unresolved count-store leads worth prioritizing if present in this tree: drivers/accel/qaic/qaic_control.c (ioctl hdr.count stored into a control message object), drivers/md/dm-era-target.c, drivers/md/dm-zoned-metadata.c, mtd/ubi fastmap count stores, bnxt TPA/ring counts, drivers/net/ethernet/google/gve/gve_adminq.c, ixgbe_e610 admin queue — read their error paths and the loops that consume the stored counts. For each candidate capture the store that poisons the field, the early return that skips reset or cleanup, and the later consuming loop with file:line; a negative result must state which error paths were walked and found clean. The seed usbip chain is demonstration-only. Save a structured artifact for snapshots/s09 only."}
